<!--
    The status-bar icon for every notification this app raises.

    ## Why it is monochrome, and why that is not a style choice

    Android draws a notification's small icon by taking its *alpha channel* and
    filling it with the system accent colour, discarding every colour in the
    source. A launcher icon used here is therefore rendered as a solid white
    blob — the classic "why is my notification icon a white square" — because
    a full-colour PNG is opaque everywhere.

    So the shape is carried entirely by transparency: white where the glyph is,
    nothing anywhere else.

    ## Why a trophy

    Every notification that reaches a closed phone today is a tournament
    check-in, and the trophy is the mark the tournament screens already use
    (`TrophyMark`). If a second kind of push is ever added, this becomes the
    app's general mark and each notification picks its own large icon.

    ## Why a vector rather than five PNGs

    A VectorDrawable scales to every density from one file, and the framework
    has accepted one as a notification small icon since API 21 — comfortably
    below this app's floor.

    Canvas is 24dp, matching Material's icon grid, so the glyph lands at the
    same optical weight as the system's own icons beside it.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24"
    android:tint="#FFFFFFFF">

    <!-- The cup: a bowl tapering to a stem, with a handle either side. -->
